PUBLIC RESPONSE: {Building on the applicant-pool and migration-adjusted points from colleagues 2, 5, 8, 9, 13 and 14, the enrollment-rate gains cited in B look more incremental than decisive once actual progression and repeater effects are factored in; net first-time entrant growth has stayed modest even as the raw 18-year-old numbers fell. Similarly, the main wave of establishment-standard easing and subsidy expansion under E was largely completed by the early 2000s, after which new approvals tightened and support shifted toward stabilizing existing institutions rather than fueling broad new capacity.  

By contrast, the demographic trajectory in A shows a milder effective drop once high-school completion and internal-migration patterns are included, adult re-entry (C) has added steady though secondary head-count especially at the graduate and extension level, and the newer professional/vocational tracks plus two-year programs (D) have functioned as stepping-stones that ultimately channel students into four-year rolls. These three elements align better with the observed stability in total entrants through 2024.  

The stronger data-driven case therefore continues to point to B and E as the less persuasive explanations.}

